Probe diff by using it, not by asking its version.

WHY: configure:194 is `if ! diff --version >/dev/null; then die "diff
missing"`. busybox's diff HAS NO --version, so the probe fails on a system
where diff works perfectly:

    diff: unrecognized option '--version'
    BusyBox v1.36.1 ... Usage: diff [-abBdiNqrTstw] ...
    diff missing: Try installing diffutils via your package manager.

THE ADVICE IS WRONG FOR THIS SYSTEM AND THE PROBE IS WRONG IN GENERAL. libvpx
uses diff exactly once outside this check -- build/make/version.sh:77:

    diff $$.tmp ${out_file} >/dev/null 2>&1 || cat $$.tmp > ${out_file}

A two-file compare with output discarded, which busybox implements. So the
package's actual requirement is met and only the version probe disagrees.

`diff /dev/null /dev/null` TESTS THE THING THAT MATTERS. It exits 0 on GNU
diffutils and on busybox, and non-zero if diff is absent or broken -- which is
what the check was trying to establish. Asking for --version tests which
implementation is installed, and libvpx does not care.

WHY NOT PACKAGE diffutils. It is not pinned, not mirrored and not in the
sysroot, and adding an artifact to satisfy a version string for a tool already
present is a large answer to a small problem. If something later needs GNU diff
semantics that busybox lacks, that is when diffutils earns a pin.

Generated with `diff -u`, not written by hand.

--- a/configure
+++ b/configure
@@ -191,7 +191,7 @@
     [ -f "${source_path}/${t}.mk" ] && enable_feature ${t}
 done
 
-if ! diff --version >/dev/null; then
+if ! diff /dev/null /dev/null >/dev/null 2>&1; then
   die "diff missing: Try installing diffutils via your package manager."
 fi
 
